.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1216 PM PST Sun Nov 22 2026
(This evening through Monday night)

A cold front passed through the region last night into the morning,
and post-frontal showers continue across much of the Central Coast
into the southern and eastern portions of Santa Clara County. Expect
the coverage of these cells to decrease through the afternoon and
evening hours, finally coming to an end overnight. Elsewhere, expect
cloudy conditions to prevail through the evening, with possible
clearing overnight. High temperatures will range from the middle to
upper 50s across the lower elevations, into the middle 40s to lower
50s across the higher elevations. Lows will range from the middle
30s to lower 40s inland, and the middle 40s to near 50 at the
coastal regions. Winds will be relatively light and rather variable
in the post-frontal environment.

Monday will represent a respite from the parade of storms, being
mainly dry if remaining a little cool with the inland valleys
hovering in the middle 50s to lower 60s. Use this day to prepare
your properties for the next storm, including clearing storm drains,
cleaning gutters, and making sure emergency kits are stocked.

Major coastal flooding concerns persist around high tide as the
combination of high astronomical tides (the first in a sequence of
"King Tides" around November through January) and surge residual
from thermal expansion and storm surge effects leads to flooding
concerns across low-lying areas of the Bayshore areas and the
Pacific Coast. A Coastal Flood Warning comes into effect for
Monday`s high tide cycle for the San Francisco and San Pablo Bays,
while the Pacific coast and Monterey Bay remain in a Coastal Flood
Advisory, all through the high tide cycle on Thursday. See the
BEACHES section for more details.

