.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1216 PM PST Sun Nov 22 2026
(Tuesday through next Saturday)

The next system arrives on Tuesday and Wednesday as a second upper
level trough develops in the northern Pacific and approaches the
Pacific Northwest with a cold front that extends south into
California. The trend towards a less impactful system in the model
output continues, particularly for inland regions and the Central
Coast. The main European and American models are now aligned on the
moisture plume coming in steeper as it approaches the coast,
resulting in the heaviest rains being pulled offshore and a gap
between the moisture pulses. As it stands, rainfall totals fall
between 1 to 2 inches in the North Bay (a little over 2 inches in
the Sonoma coastal ranges), 0.25 to 1 inch across the Bay Area and
Monterey Bay (higher totals landing closer to the coast, with rain
totals slightly over 1 inch in the Santa Cruz mountains and 0.5 to 1
inch in the Santa Lucia range) and 0.1 to 0.3 inches in the interior
Central Coast. Wind gusts will peak around 20 to 30 mph throughout
the region, with gusts to 40 mph possible along the coast and
through favored gaps and passes. There`s also a slight chances of
thunderstorms associated with the main frontal band, where locally
heavy downpours and gusty winds could develop if all the right
factors align. A couple notes about the impacts: first, this system
roughly coincides with the first cycle of the King Tides, and rain
runoff that reaches the Bays to find historically high tide levels
may contribute to coastal flooding impacts. Second, this system will
also impact the busy Thanksgiving travel period and depending on how
the system plays out, travel delays on roads, railways, and
airplanes are possible. Finally, the strong low pressure offshore
will likely generate strong swells that increase the risk of
hazardous conditions along the Pacific Coast beaches.

As the system clears out late Wednesday into early Thursday, the
Thanksgiving holiday itself should be generally dry, with an
incoming ridge helping to warm the inland valleys into the middle to
upper 60s. Another chance for rain arrives late Friday into the
upcoming weekend. Ensemble model cluster analysis begins to split
as varying solutions become present in the ensemble space, making
the forecast highly uncertain. To give an example, for the period
from the morning of Saturday the 28th to the morning of Monday the
30th, NBM guidance suggests that there is an 80 percent probability
that rainfall in downtown San Francisco falls between a few
hundredths of an inch to 1.6 inches, and a 50 percent probability
that rainfall ranges between 0.3 to 1.0 inches. Beyond the seven-
day outlook, the storm door looks to remain open with ensemble model
clusters supporting a chance for another system to come in within
the first couple days of December.

