.AVIATION...
(18Z TAFS)
Issued at 909 AM PST Sun Nov 22 2026

A weak cold front pushed through the Bay Area this morning,
leaving widely scattered showers and mid to high overcast skies.
The shower activity will decrease through the day as the clouds
gradually clear. On Monday another round of high clouds will move
in ahead of a much stronger cold front on Tuesday. The wind
forecast is a challenge in the middle of these systems as a
building southerly gradient battles the typical diurnal wind
forcing.

Vicinity of SFO...MVFR conditions will persist for a couple more
hours before lifting. Winds were a little stronger than expected
mid-morning, with NE winds greater than 10 kts. There is a lot of
uncertainty with both the wind direction and strength over the
next 4 to 6 hours. Models are suggesting a return to northerly
winds by 21Z, but they didn`t capture the current strength of the
NE winds. That discrepancy indicates they may have more staying
power. Typically winds shift to NW in the afternoon, but with the
building southerly gradient, there is a chance they remain NE
through the day and shift to SE overnight. Even if the winds dont
flip back onshore, the strength will certainly decrease as the
daily onshore gradient at least weakens what`s going on now.

SFO Bridge Approach...Similar to SFO.

Monterey Bay Terminals...A couple brief, light showers are still
possible at both MRY and SNS, but the window is closing as drier
air filters in. Again winds are tricky here, with a stronger
offshore gradient than normal. SNS will likely flip back onshore
in the afternoon before going back down valley tonight.

